-1$","$1$","Undefined"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"62b29ccc94502c664ddd47ed","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_dirichlet_function_07e83b91_009_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is the Dirichlet function described as extremely irregular?","options":["It is discontinuous at rational points only, a dense set of jumps.","Every interval holds rationals and irrationals, so values jump between $0$ and $1$.","Rationals and irrationals alternate on the line, so the value switches at each next point.","It equals $1$ on a dense set, so its Riemann integral over $[0,1]$ equals $1$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d858daf8ef03669b9a3e24b9","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_dirichlet_function_07e83b91_011_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Is the Dirichlet function Riemann integrable on $[0,1]$?","options":["Yes, because it is bounded on $[0,1]$.","Yes, because it takes only the values $0$ and $1$.","Yes, with integral $0$, because $[0,1]$ has far fewer rationals than irrationals.","No: every upper sum on $[0,1]$ is $1$ and every lower sum is $0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6dfe3c88c4be5c288f58c7f5","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_dirichlet_function_07e83b91_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f=\\mathbb{1}_{\\mathbb{Q}}$ be the Dirichlet function, with $f(x)=1$ for rational $x$ and $f(x)=0$ for irrational $x$. What is $f(f(x))$ for a real number $x$?","options":["$f(x)$ for every real $x$","$1-f(x)$ for every real $x$","$0$ for every real $x$","$1$ for every real $x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"035f66ec32021c59db4af6e0","time_limit":40}]}}